<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US Surgeon General reports that smoking causes cancer</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Luther Terry's advisory committee concluded that cigarette smoking causes lung cancer in men. The report led to health warnings on cigarette packets and decades of anti-smoking policy.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-9">9 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Beatles appear on 'The Ed Sullivan Show'</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">An estimated 73 million Americans watched the Beatles' first live US television appearance. The broadcast launched the 'British Invasion' of American popular music.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-14">14 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Jack Ruby convicted of murdering Lee Harvey Oswald</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A Dallas jury found the nightclub owner guilty of shooting Kennedy's accused assassin on live television and sentenced him to death. The verdict was overturned, and Ruby died before a retrial.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-12">12 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Nelson Mandela is sentenced to life imprisonment</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Mandela and seven co-accused in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rivonia-trial">Rivonia Trial</a> were sentenced to life for sabotage. He spent 27 years in prison before his release in 1990.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-21">21 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Three civil rights workers are murdered in Mississippi</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">James Chaney, Andrew Goodman and Michael Schwerner were killed by Klansmen, with the involvement of local law officers, during Freedom Summer. Their deaths spurred support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Lyndon Johnson signs the Civil Rights Act</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">President Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act, outlawing discrimination on the basis of race, colour, religion, sex or national origin. It ended legal segregation in public places and was the most sweeping civil rights law since Reconstruction.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-4">4 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US destroyers report a second attack in the Gulf of Tonkin</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">US warships reported an attack by North Vietnamese boats that probably never happened. Within days Congress passed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, authorising Johnson to escalate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-27">27 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Warren Commission report is made public</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The commission's report concluded that Lee Harvey Oswald alone had killed President Kennedy. Its findings have been widely debated ever since.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-14">14 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Soviet leaders oust Nikita Khrushchev</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Recalled from holiday, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> was forced out by the Communist Party's Central Committee. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a> succeeded him as party leader and steered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> into two decades of stability and stagnation.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-22">22 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The SR-71 Blackbird makes its first flight</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Lockheed's SR-71 reconnaissance aircraft first flew at Palmdale, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. Able to cruise above Mach 3 at about 25,000 metres, it remains the fastest crewed jet-powered aircraft.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolution</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/zanzibar-revolution">Zanzibar Revolution</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Zanzibar Revolution of 12 January 1964 overthrew the sultan and the government led by the Zanzibar Nationalist Party a month after independence.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/barotseland-agreement-1964">Barotseland Agreement 1964</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Barotseland Agreement 1964 was signed in May 1964 by Kenneth Kaunda for Northern Rhodesia, the Litunga Mwanawina III and the British government.</p></div></li></ol></section>
